<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catalan Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1302">1302</a>–<a href="/years/1388">1388</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Grand Catalan Company, Almogavars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catalan Company was a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, mostly Catalan and Aragonese almogavars, led first by Roger de Flor and hired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1303 against the Turks. After quarrelling with Byzantium it ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia, then defeated and killed Duke Walter of Brienne at Halmyros in 1311 and ruled the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> until 1388.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catalan-company.jpg" alt="Catalan Company" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramon Muntaner. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cr%C3%B2nica_de_Ramon_Muntaner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The almogavars were elite light infantry formed on the Iberian peninsula in the 13th century by the demands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, and the Crown of Aragon used them in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ventures, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a>. They fought in companies of 20 to 50 men under a chief of proven skill. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-caltabellotta">Peace of Caltabellotta</a> of 1302 left them without employment, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-aragon">James II of Aragon</a> and Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> feared the threat they posed to order. Many were therefore gathered into a company of companies under Roger de Flor, to be paid in Byzantine service.</p><p>The Company left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> with 36 ships carrying about 8,000 men, by the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-muntaner">Ramon Muntaner</a>, although the Byzantine historian Nicephorus Gregoras gives only 1,000. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a> married Roger de Flor to his niece Maria Asanina and named him Megas Doux. The newcomers angered the Genoese, and some 3,000 of them were killed in the Genoese massacre of September 1303.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> the Company defeated the Karasid Turks at the Battle of the Cyzicus in October 1303, relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> after the Battle of Aulax in 1304 and won the Battle of Kibistra on 15 August 1304. Greek historians recorded heavy looting and abuse of the Byzantine population by the almogavars. On 30 April 1305 the emperor's son Michael IX Palaiologos had Alan mercenaries murder Roger de Flor at Adrianople, and the Catalans answered by killing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> and defeating Michael at the Battle of Apros in July 1305.</p><p>After quarrels among its leaders, in which Bernat de Rocafort was arrested and starved to death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1309, the Company moved west. It pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> by 1309. Its leader Roger Deslaur then took service with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-v-of-brienne">Walter V of Brienne</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, whose refusal to pay led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros">Battle of Halmyros</a> on 15 March 1311.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Company held the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> into the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a>, ruling as feudal lords. In 1312 it accepted the overlordship of the Aragonese crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and took a seal with the head of St George, and in 1318 the Pope excommunicated its members for refusing to give the duchies back to their heirs. It held them until it was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a> and the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli">Nerio I Acciaioli</a> of Corinth. Muntaner later wrote a chronicle of its history, and the monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> barred Catalans from the mountain until 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catalan Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Light infantry known as almogavers fought in Sicily, and as the <b>Catalan Company</b> they served the Byzantine emperor from 1303 before seizing the Duchy of Athens after the Battle of Halmyros in 1311.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Roger de Flor, commander of the <b>Catalan Company</b>, mercenaries hired by Byzantine Emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos against the Anatolian beyliks, was murdered.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The result kept Achaea under Angevin supremacy and checked Ferdinand's allies, the <b>Catalan Company</b> occupying the Duchy of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-5">5 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas III d'Autremencourt was killed in 1311 fighting the <b>Catalan Company</b> at the Battle of Halmyros.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-salona">County of Salona</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1311 the <b>Catalan Company</b> killed Duke Walter of Brienne at Halmyros and ruled the duchy for the Crown of Aragon and Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Catalan Company</b> conquered the Duchy of Athens in 1311, it pushed north into Thessaly.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-neopatras">Duchy of Neopatras</a></li></ul></section>
